{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '170,199p' paper.tex; sed -n '233,258p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"\\subsection{Finite-Audit Evidence Does Not Establish an Accuracy Gain}\nFor the real sixteen-prompt conditions, average audit accuracy of the selected prompt is $12.031\\%$ at $n=64$ and $11.641\\%$ at $n=256$. These modest values concern this small model with constrained direct answers; they do not characterize larger LLMs or alternative decoding procedures. The report and reuse conditions share that accuracy by construction. Their estimation-error contrast cannot establish that protection produces a better system.\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/condition_means/R64K16/selected_accuracy]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/condition_means/R256K16/selected_accuracy]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/real_audit_accuracy_difference.pdf}\n\\caption{Selected-minus-fixed audit accuracy for each seed at $K=16$. Points use paired outcomes on the same audit; lines are the recorded subject-bootstrap percentile intervals. Zero is included in eight of the ten intervals. Both positive exclusions occur for seed $5059$, not independent benchmark replications. Intervals are descriptive, not simultaneous, and do not quantify population coverage of the reporting rule.}\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/audit_differences]]\n\\label{fig:audit}\n\\end{figure}\n\nFigure~\\ref{fig:audit} asks the separate question of whether the selected prompt is more accurate than the default prompt. Eight of ten recorded intervals include zero; the two positive exclusions both belong to seed $5059$. This is not evidence of a consistent accuracy gain, and the overlapping question pools preclude reading those exclusions as two independent confirmations. For $K=1$ the selected and default prompts coincide, giving identically zero paired differences. These controls help distinguish honest reporting from improved selection.\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/audit_differences]]\n\nAll ten real sixteen-prompt Wilson intervals contain their respective finite-audit accuracies. Across the remaining real conditions, one of the single-prompt intervals fails to contain the audit reference. Neither observation estimates 95\\% population coverage with meaningful precision: the number of realized settings is small, the audit accuracy is noisy, and settings share data. Likewise, a lower audit-reference error may reflect both estimation quality and the particular audit realization. We therefore keep the real findings at the level of measured audit agreement.\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/condition_means]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/condition_results.csv]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What a reported number should identify.}\nThe evidence supports documenting three objects together: the selected configuration, the observations used for selection and reporting, and the target against which reliability is assessed. A reused selection score describes an observation after maximization; a protected score describes the same configuration using other observations. A full-budget score may describe another configuration entirely. An interval around any of these numbers does not, by itself, settle which comparison is appropriate. This is why the strongest matched reporting comparison and the strongest error counterexamples both belong in the main results.\n\n\\paragraph{What the study does not isolate.}\nThe same-winner contrast isolates the reporting-row substitution within a fixed split. It does not isolate every effect of an end-to-end optimization pipeline. Fixed versus searched prompts, randomized versus alternative splits, and full-budget versus half-budget selection change multiple quantities. The bank is frozen, with no outcome-dependent prompt generation, repeated release of holdout feedback, or independent assessment of a learned search policy. Unmeasured allocation rules and unavailable ablations cannot support component-level conclusions. No result demonstrates that a report set remains protected after repeated human or automated inspection.\n\n\\paragraph{Question populations and dependence.}\nThe known-truth construction uses independent question rows and specified prompt dependence. Public benchmark questions need not follow that model. The real audit is held apart from selection within its condition, but it is a finite sample from a shared public pool. Overlap across seeds, unmeasured difficulty structure, possible training contamination, and semantic duplication beyond normalization all limit population interpretation. The subject bootstrap preserves paired within-question comparisons but has few draws and no demonstrated calibration here. Neither the bootstrap intervals nor the aggregate containment indicator should be advertised as a validated deployment guarantee.\n\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\subsection{Complete Seed-Level Results}\nTable~\\ref{tab:seed} gives every recorded condition. Error values are multiplied by $10^3$; target accuracy, coverage, and interval width remain on the unit scale. Synthetic targets and diagnostics average four regimes with $1024$ replicates each. A real row has a single audit-reference error and a binary containment indicator, so a value of one is not an estimate of certain population coverage. The machine-readable \\texttt{analysis/condition\\_results.csv} additionally includes all six comparator errors, signed errors, and recorded diagnostics. \\texttt{analysis/regime\\_results.csv} preserves the full regime-level metrics and supplied report-MSE standard errors; these marginal standard errors are not used as errors for paired method differences. \\texttt{analysis/summary.json} supplies equal-weight condition and regime means without additional scientific evaluations.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\\begin{center}\n\\small\n\\captionof{table}{All seed-level conditions. Report and Reuse are squared-error metrics $\\times10^3$. Target is selected-prompt known accuracy for synthetic rows and finite-audit accuracy for real rows. Cov. denotes population coverage only for synthetic rows; for real rows it denotes audit containment.}\n\\label{tab:seed}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\\begin{longtable}{rrlrrrrrr}\\toprule\nSeed & $n$ & Setting & $K$ & Report & Reuse & Target & Cov. & Width\\\\\\midrule\\endhead\n1009 & 64 & Synth. & 1 & 7.6179 & 7.4306 & 0.5000 & 0.9519 & 0.3169\\\\\n1009 & 64 & Synth. & 16 & 6.0695 & 11.9401 & 0.6842 & 0.9597 & 0.2964\\\\\n1009 & 256 & Synth. & 1 & 1.8620 & 1.9005 & 0.5000 & 0.9536 & 0.1667\\\\\n1009 & 256 & Synth. & 16 & 1.5774 & 2.7214 & 0.6951 & 0.9517 & 0.1528\\\\\n1009 & 64 & Real & 1 & 3.6659 & 0.0038 & 0.1230 & 1.0000 & 0.1842\\\\\n1009 & 64 & Real & 16 & 0.3815 & 1.8463 & 0.1133 & 1.0000 & 0.2098\\\\\n1009 & 256 & Real & 1 & 0.3815 & 0.3815 & 0.1133 & 1.0000 & 0.1178\\\\\n1009 & 256 & Real & 16 & 0.7477 & 3.4332 & 0.1133 & 1.0000 & 0.0987\\\\\n2027 & 64 & Synth. & 1 & 7.4230 & 7.3855 & 0.5000 & 0.9531 & 0.3168\\\\\n2027 & 64 & Synth. & 16 & 6.3961 & 11.7112 & 0.6843 & 0.9473 & 0.2962\\\\\n2027 & 256 & Synth. & 1 & 1.8362 & 1.8925 & 0.5000 & 0.9570 & 0.1667\\\\\n2027 & 256 & Synth. & 16 & 1.6153 & 2.8066 & 0.6947 & 0.9514 & 0.1529\\\\\n2027 & 64 & Real & 1 & 0.5493 & 13.7329 & 0.1016 & 1.0000 & 0.2310\\\\\n2027 & 64 & Real & 16 & 2.1973 & 19.7754 & 0.1094 & 1.0000 & 0.2489\\\\\n2027 & 256 & Real & 1 & 4.4098 & 6.7291 & 0.0820 & 0.0000 & 0.1231\\\\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '287,345p' paper.tex; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom collections import Counter\nclaims=json.load(open('claims_grounding.json'))['claims']\nprint('\\\\nCLAIM SCHEMA', list(claims[0]))\nfor claim in claims[:3]: print(json.dumps(claim,ensure_ascii=False)[:1500])\nprint('CLAIM TYPES',Counter(claim.get('claim_type',claim.get('type')) for claim in claims))\nstrategy=json.load(open('paper_strategy.json'))\nfor key in ['central_question','central_thesis','edit_framing_decision','notes_for_writer']:\n print('STRATEGY',key, json.dumps(strategy[key],ensure_ascii=False))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '287,345p' paper.tex; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom collections import Counter\nclaims=json.load(open('claims_grounding.json'))['claims']\nprint('\\\\nCLAIM SCHEMA', list(claims[0]))\nfor claim in claims[:3]: print(json.dumps(claim,ensure_ascii=False)[:1500])\nprint('CLAIM TYPES',Counter(claim.get('claim_type',claim.get('type')) for claim in claims))\nstrategy=json.load(open('paper_strategy.json'))\nfor key in ['central_question','central_thesis','edit_framing_decision','notes_for_writer']:\n print('STRATEGY',key, json.dumps(strategy[key],ensure_ascii=False))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\\subsection{Partition, Estimand, and Interval Details}\nThe default instruction is: ``Answer the multiple-choice question. Return only the correct option letter A through J.'' The remaining bank entries are fixed paraphrases requesting a single capital letter without explanation. The complete bank is inspectable in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/}\\texttt{program/src/program.py}, lines 55--76. The same file's reset and update handlers, lines 78--102, implement the seeded shuffle, half partition, uniform weights, and fixed priority. The preserved baseline and studied policy files are byte-identical; they are not counted as distinct scientific comparators.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L70]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py]]\n\nFor report mean $\\widehat p$, count $m=|R|$, and $z=1.959963984540054$, the implemented Wilson interval has center and half-width\n\\begin{equation}\n C=\\frac{\\widehat p+z^2/(2m)}{1+z^2/m},\\qquad\n H=\\frac{z\\sqrt{\\widehat p(1-\\widehat p)/m+z^2/(4m^2)}}{1+z^2/m}.\n\\end{equation}\nThe returned interval is $[C-H,C+H]$. Its use here is a specified conventional construction, not a claim of exact coverage. The selected prompt is evaluated on $m$ report rows regardless of how many prompts were considered. Comparators other than same-winner reuse may have another selected prompt, so their squared errors substitute their own corresponding reference accuracy.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L746]]\n\nFor audit subject bootstrap intervals, per-question differences are selected-prompt accuracy minus default-prompt accuracy. The evaluator samples the list of observed subjects with replacement and concatenates all question differences from every sampled subject, including multiplicities. The resulting mean is question-weighted within that resampled collection, not an equal-weight average of subject means. It takes the $0.025$ and $0.975$ empirical quantiles of $200$ draws using generator seed equal to the evaluation seed plus $76531$. This construction holds the observed selected prompt fixed; it does not repeat selection inside each bootstrap draw.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L987]]\n\n\\subsection{Diagnostic Controls and Evidence Boundaries}\nThe constant control sets all entries to zero and requires tie priority to be respected. The copied-column control repeats the default column across prompts and again checks tie priority. Neither is an empirical test of generalization. The contaminated-report control intentionally uses selection rows for reporting and verifies that reused and reported scores coincide; its independence flag is false. This is an expected detection, not a failure of the intended disjoint partition. Synthetic permutation rolls outcome rows; the real permutation changes answer assignments and checks that the change is nonvacuous. These controls do not measure every possible leakage mechanism.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L785]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L976]]\n\nThe final record independently reports completion and successful qualification. No final qualification failure is present. The absence of a scored replacement during development is separate: there are zero scored and three unscored alternative evaluations. Timeout records do not reveal those alternatives' scientific performance or identify a causal reason for missing scores. We consequently make no comparative claim about their allocation policies. Search-scope scores are neither final-scope measurements nor answer accuracies and are not used in the paper's quantitative comparisons.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/downstream_study/attempt_counts]]\n\nThe local evaluator digest equals the recorded evaluator digest. This links the inspected estimator, inference, and diagnostic definitions to a named evaluator file. It does not establish equality between every differently scoped source tree in the provenance record. Likewise, an earlier audit verdict or a file hash is not the substantive evidence for a detailed implementation claim; the readable source locations above provide that evidence. Detailed gate explanations absent from the supplied record remain unknown, and candidate-origin diagnostic annotations remain unverified.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluator_sha256]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py]]\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility and Resource Accounting}\nThe fixed model revision is \\texttt{12fd25f77366fa6b3b4b768ec3050bf629380bac}; MMLU-Pro is fixed at revision \\texttt{b189ec765aa7ed75c8acfea42df31fdae71f97be}. The retained real pool has $4096$ examples. Normalization uses Unicode NFKC and collapses whitespace; option-content matching sorts normalized options. The split manifest records $11711$ unique groups and allocation seed $732091$. These describe dataset preparation, not additional measured performance. Data may have appeared in pretraining.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/runtime_manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/split_manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/examples.json]]\n\nThe supplied dependency specification pins NumPy 2.2.6, PyArrow 19.0.1, PyTorch 2.6.0, Transformers 4.51.3, Safetensors 0.5.3, SciPy 1.15.3, and Matplotlib 3.10.3. The runtime manifest identifies CPython 3.12 on Linux x86\\_64, CPU float32 inference and float64 statistics. The answer worker uses two tensor threads and one interoperation thread. These are the supplied environment specification and inspected settings; a separately retained package inventory proving every version actually loaded is unavailable. The paper-artifact environment is recorded separately in the reproduction guide.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/requirements.txt]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/runtime_manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L574]]\n\nTo regenerate manuscript analyses without model calls, run \\texttt{python build\\_artifacts.py}. It reads the immutable final record and produces both quantitative figures as PDF and 300-DPI PNG, the main comparison table, the regime table, the complete seed table, and their evidence sidecars. It leaves an orchestrator-rendered overview untouched. The exact final-evaluation entrypoint, resource acquisition details, and validation commands are in \\texttt{REPRODUCIBILITY.md}. Evaluation commands there describe future reproduction, not additional executions during manuscript preparation. Predicted reproduction time is uncertain because the recorded durations include shared caching and orchestration overhead; the measured duration sum is the only supplied wall-time accounting.\n% @evidence[[build_artifacts.py]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/duration_sum_seconds]]\n\nReferences were consulted through fixed original arXiv abstracts, original journal pages, or original publisher-deposited abstracts and bibliographic records, as individually recorded in \\texttt{references\\_provenance.json}. No citation graph or broad topical discovery was used. Full-text reading is not claimed when only an abstract was consulted. No known encounter with the prohibited study occurred. The official ICLR 2026 style and bibliography style were obtained directly from the ICLR template repository, rather than relabeling a template for another year.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLLM-based research assistance contributed to proposal generation, implementation editing, reference planning, narrative selection, plotting code, manuscript drafting, and evidence checking. The research assistance is distinct from the evaluated answer generator, SmolLM2-135M-Instruct. Its role in evaluation is limited to producing the constrained answer letters described in the method. The writing assistant inspected the supplied results and source, generated deterministic summaries and figures, and validated the manuscript; it did not run new scientific inference or simulations for this paper. Literature retrieval used fixed identifiers for permitted originals. No unrecorded human validation, external audit, or independent replication is asserted.\n\n\\end{document}\n\nCLAIM SCHEMA ['claim', 'source_ids', 'citation_keys', 'evidence_refs']\n{\"claim\": \"Complete seed-table and machine-readable result row, seed 1009, condition S64K1: {\\\"control_failure_rate\\\": 0.0, \\\"coverage\\\": 0.951904296875, \\\"decision_reversal_rate\\\": 0.0, \\\"failure_rate\\\": 0.0, \\\"fixed_prompt_mse\\\": 0.003808543682098389, \\\"full_budget_naive_mse\\\": 0.003808543682098389, \\\"honest_half_mse\\\": 0.007740268707275391, \\\"interval_width\\\": 0.31687861103992715, \\\"random_prompt_mse\\\": 0.003808543682098389, \\\"report_bias\\\": -0.00043487548828125, \\\"report_mse\\\": 0.007617864608764648, \\\"reuse_mse\\\": 0.007430610656738282, \\\"selected_accuracy\\\": 0.5, \\\"signed_optimism\\\": 0.00146484375}.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0\", \"analysis/condition_results.csv\", \"tables/seed_results.tex\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Complete seed-table and machine-readable result row, seed 1009, condition S64K16: {\\\"control_failure_rate\\\": 0.0, \\\"coverage\\\": 0.959716796875, \\\"decision_reversal_rate\\\": 0.000732421875, \\\"failure_rate\\\": 0.0, \\\"fixed_prompt_mse\\\": 0.003661665916442871, \\\"full_budget_naive_mse\\\": 0.005447328111860487, \\\"honest_half_mse\\\": 0.006518946880764431, \\\"interval_width\\\": 0.29644363988131484, \\\"random_prompt_mse\\\": 0.0032765861405266656, \\\"report_bias\\\": -0.0010075887044270974, \\\"report_mse\\\": 0.006069530275132921, \\\"reuse_mse\\\": 0.011940063264634874, \\\"selected_accuracy\\\": 0.6842122395833333, \\\"signed_optimism\\\": 0.0725550333658854}.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/1\", \"analysis/condition_results.csv\", \"tables/seed_results.tex\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Complete seed-table and machine-readable result row, seed 1009, condition S256K1: {\\\"control_failure_rate\\\": 0.0, \\\"coverage\\\": 0.95361328125, \\\"decision_reversal_rate\\\": 0.0, \\\"failure_rate\\\": 0.0, \\\"fixed_prompt_mse\\\": 0.0009485287964344025, \\\"full_budget_naive_mse\\\": 0.0009485287964344025, \\\"honest_half_mse\\\": 0.0019209212064743041, \\\"interval_width\\\": 0.16671460658773965, \\\"random_prompt_mse\\\": 0.0009485287964344025, \\\"report_bias\\\": 0.00018310546875, \\\"report_mse\\\": 0.0018619811534881592, \\\"reuse_mse\\\": 0.0019004708528518677, \\\"selected_accuracy\\\": 0.5, \\\"signed_optimism\\\": -0.0012836456298828125}.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/2\", \"analysis/condition_results.csv\", \"tables/seed_results.tex\"]}\nCLAIM TYPES Counter({None: 127})\nSTRATEGY central_question \"When does reserving benchmark questions for reporting reduce error after prompt selection, and what can known-truth simulations versus finite real-model audits establish about reliability?\"\nSTRATEGY central_thesis \"Across the five final seeds, half-split reporting lowers mean error relative to reusing the same winner's selection score in the sixteen-prompt conditions, but does not uniformly beat full-budget or non-search comparators, and the real-model results establish audit agreement rather than population validity.\"\nSTRATEGY edit_framing_decision \"No previous_paper directory, resolved edit-context file, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory is present; this is fresh framing, not a manuscript-preservation decision. The recentered label denotes a move from the task's broad automatic-tuning ambition and private proposal history to the frozen baseline's measured reporting trade-off. There is no prior manuscript title or figure plan to preserve.\"\nSTRATEGY notes_for_writer [\"Ground quantitative claims in final.metric.* or final.evidence.episodes, qualification in final.qualification.*, and selection, search differences, and attempt accounting in study.*. Never substitute downstream_study.final_evaluation for the independent final record. If authoritative qualification changes to not_qualified, disclose it in the body, review, audit and revision; identify a cause only if evaluator evidence establishes it. The current source says qualified, not failed.\", \"Private-only pipeline context: the retained search score equals its baseline score and has zero delta; these are not accuracy measurements or final-scope improvements. Do not put those internal score labels into visible figures. The baseline is the studied policy; do not manufacture a second identical baseline series.\", \"Problem formalization should distinguish a prompt selected from a frozen bank, its conditional fresh-question accuracy, the reused selection mean, the protected report mean, and the finite audit estimate. Reporting MSE and selected-prompt accuracy answer different questions. Full-budget, fixed, random, and alternative-half methods have their own prompt targets, so their MSE differences do not isolate a single mechanism.\", \"The real setting is HuggingFaceTB/SmolLM2-135M-Instruct revision 12fd25f77366fa6b3b4b768ec3050bf629380bac on MMLU-Pro revision b189ec765aa7ed75c8acfea42df31fdae71f97be, CPU float32, deterministic constrained one-letter generation. Do not replace it with an assumed 7B model, chain-of-thought optimizer, DSPy implementation, or free-form response task. Public-data pretraining contamination is possible, not established.\", \"The four synthetic regimes cross equal/graded means and shared-outcome dependence; one prompt has no selection multiplicity. All source-defined conditions remain visible. The cross-family aggregate uses equal case/seed/regime weights rather than pooling synthetic replicates with real examples. Derive any display summaries reproducibly from paper_data.json and label weighting explicitly; do not introduce ungrounded numeric confidence intervals.\", \"Observed control completion, zero replay discrepancy, and qualified status do not imply representative sampling, verified candidate diagnostics, adaptation-safe inference, or reliable repeated-population coverage. The copy-report control is deliberately contaminated and correctly records false independence. The decision-reversal metric uses protected-report-minus-full-sample-fixed versus selected-target-minus-fixed-target signs, excludes ties, and is not the probability that a naive claim is overturned; omit it from the main story unless its exact estimand is explained.\", \"Use one common research question with separate evidential roles: known-truth simulation for bias/coverage and real inference for finite-audit agreement and prompt-accuracy uncertainty. Do not merge them into a single population coverage headline. Real audit pools overlap across settings and derive from a shared public pool; seed scatter is descriptive, not evidence for independent datasets or repeated training runs.\", \"Body target is 8.5 pages within VENUE.md's 8-9-page limit, excluding references: 1.25 pages for title/abstract/problem/contributions and the early overview; 0.75 related work; 1.5 estimands and reporting policies; 1.0 design/provenance; 2.0 controlled comparisons with Figure 2 and the all-comparator table; 0.75 real audit uncertainty with Figure 3; 1.25 interpretation, limitations, reproducibility pointer and conclusion. These are layout allocations, not measured results. A body of exactly 8 pages requires its last page at least 75% full. Spend space on target distinctions, regime-wise uncertainty and counterexamples rather than larger figures, repeated prose or new experiments.\", \"Suggested body headings: Introduction; Related Work; Selection and Reporting Targets; Reporting Policies; Experimental Design; Reporting Error and Selection Multiplicity; Finite-Audit Evidence; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Include a compact main table of all six comparators and a synthetic regime diagnostic table covering report bias, reuse optimism, coverage and interval width. The negative comparisons and finite-audit caveat remain in the body, not only the supplement.\", \"Inspect and retain paper.sty's practical single-column geometry: 5.5 inches text width by 9 inches height. In this format column-width and full-width both span the one text column; the full_width figure designation means that span, not a two-column float. The local style has historical comments, and the staged task template is named iclr2027_conference while VENUE.md mandates ICLR 2026. Do not silently advertise the 2027 template as official 2026. Before final drafting/format validation, verify the required 2026 style provenance or obtain the exact official 2026 files through a direct known official source without prohibited-literature discovery; preserve anonymization and do not change geometry to force fit.\", \"Place Supplementary Information after references in the same manuscript using clearpage, bibliography, clearpage, appendix, section Supplementary Information. Include the mandatory LLM Usage subsection, substantive full results and reproduction details. Route separately supplied appendix-ranked analyses there; do not guess or modify their ranking here.\", \"Quantitative figures must be reproducible from local plotting source, exported as vector PDF and PNG at 300 DPI, with LaTeX referencing PDF whenever available. At full 5.5-inch span use at least 2400-pixel width for raster-only assets; any column inset uses at least 1200 pixels. Never upscale existing raster output. Retain editable concept-figure source and prompt provenance; at most one overview uses generate-concept-figure. Record final dimensions, placement and minimum rendered font in the later figure manifest.\", \"References are a bibliography/provenance pool, not evidence that documents were read or that retrieved items are relevant. Do not pad related work with incidental retrieval matches. The permitted starter exact-title scope and retrieved Berk2013ValidPI provide positioning leads only after original-document consultation is verified; never invent missing MMLU-Pro, Wilson or bootstrap citations. Do not search for the forbidden paper, its identity, citation graph, summaries, or reproductions; stop and log any known accidental encounter. No external literature browsing was performed by this framing agent.\", \"Do not claim paired certificates, outcome-dependent correction, quarter allocation, stratum-balanced gains, adaptive-search guarantees, unseen models, theoretical novelty, or completed ablations. Those are not measured interventions in the final evidence. No new inference, simulations, final paper, claim ledger, or final-paper validation belongs in this stage.\", \"Ready means the artifacts support a bounded standalone strategy, not that they establish main-conference novelty, acceptance, statistical superiority, or completed reproduction. The template-year mismatch and precise citation consultation are explicit downstream production checks, not permission to dilute the scientific limitations.\"]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
